-- Q12083: Intangible Assets [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only in a business combination, as the excess of purchase price over identifiable net asset fair value","When internally generated goodwill becomes reliably measurable through valuation models","Whenever market capitalization exceeds the book value of net equity on the balance sheet","When management estimates the entity''s reputation value and expected future synergies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 805-30-25-1, goodwill is recognized only in a business combination as the excess of consideration transferred over the fair value of identifiable net assets acquired. Internally generated goodwill is never capitalized under U.S. GAAP.
Wrong (B): U.S. GAAP prohibits recognition of internally generated goodwill regardless of measurement reliability.
Wrong (C): Market capitalization exceeding book value may suggest unrecognized intangible value, but it does not trigger goodwill recognition.
Wrong (D): Management estimates of reputation and synergies do not qualify for goodwill recognition outside a business combination.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12083;

-- Q12091: Inventory [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As a selling expense reported on the income statement for the period","As an inventoriable cost added to the raw materials purchase price","As a general and administrative expense of the current period","As a separate intangible asset on the balance sheet"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 330-10-30, all costs necessary to bring inventory to its present location and condition are inventoriable. Freight-in on purchased materials is capitalized as part of inventory cost, making the total $63,500.
Wrong (A): Freight-in is not a selling expense; freight-out (delivery to customers) is classified as a selling cost.
Wrong (C): Inbound freight directly relates to inventory acquisition, not general administration.
Wrong (D): Freight charges are not intangible assets; they are a component of the tangible inventory cost.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12091;

-- Q12119: Inventory [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Debit Inventory $16,000 and credit Sales Revenue $16,000 to offset the decline","Debit Cost of Goods Sold or Loss $16,000 and credit Inventory or an allowance account $16,000","Debit Other Comprehensive Income $16,000 and credit Inventory $16,000 as unrealized","No entry is needed because FIFO inventory must always remain at its historical cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 330-10-35 (as amended by ASU 2015-11), FIFO inventory must be written down when NRV falls below cost. The $16,000 loss ($125,000 minus $109,000) is charged to COGS or a separate loss account with a corresponding credit to Inventory or a contra-asset allowance.
Wrong (A): An inventory write-down is not credited to Sales Revenue; it is a cost or loss recognition, not a revenue transaction.
Wrong (C): Inventory write-downs under ASC 330 flow through the income statement, not other comprehensive income.
Wrong (D): The lower-of-cost-or-NRV rule requires a write-down when NRV is below cost, even under FIFO.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12119;

-- Q12156: Payables and Accrued Liabilities [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both warranties are accrued as liabilities using estimated costs at the point of sale","Both warranties are separate performance obligations with revenue deferred over the coverage period","The standard warranty is accrued as a cost; the extended warranty is a separate performance obligation","The standard warranty is expensed as incurred; the extended warranty is accrued as an estimated liability"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 606-10-25-1, an assurance-type warranty covering defects is not a separate performance obligation; estimated costs are accrued as a liability under ASC 460. A service-type warranty sold separately is a distinct performance obligation, and the $120 price is deferred as unearned revenue recognized over the coverage period.
Wrong (A): This fails to distinguish the revenue deferral required for the extended warranty, which is a separate performance obligation.
Wrong (B): The standard warranty is an assurance-type warranty and does not create a separate performance obligation.
Wrong (D): Standard warranty costs are accrued when the product is sold, not expensed as claims are incurred.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12156;

-- Q12163: Payables and Accrued Liabilities [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The deferred tax liability arising from temporary differences between book and tax bases","The cumulative income tax expense recognized since the entity''s inception date","The estimated future tax obligation on currently unrealized gains in the portfolio","The current-period income tax owed to taxing authorities that has not yet been remitted"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 740-10-25, income tax payable is a current liability representing taxes owed on the current period''s taxable income that remain unpaid at the balance sheet date. It reflects amounts due to federal, state, or local authorities.
Wrong (A): Deferred tax liabilities arise from temporary differences under ASC 740-10-25-20 and are separate from current taxes owed.
Wrong (B): Income tax payable reflects only the current period obligation, not a cumulative lifetime amount.
Wrong (C): Estimated future taxes on unrealized gains relate to deferred tax accounting, not current taxes payable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12163;

-- Q12164: Payables and Accrued Liabilities [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An amount withheld from progress billings until project completion or satisfaction of terms","A penalty assessed against the contractor for late delivery of construction materials","An advance payment made by the owner to secure the contractor''s availability for the project","A performance bond posted by the general contractor to guarantee workmanship quality"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 606-10-45-3 and construction industry practice, retention (retainage) payable represents amounts withheld from contractor billings, typically 5% to 10%, until specified milestones or project completion. The withheld amount is a liability of the party holding it.
Wrong (B): Retention is not a penalty; it is a contractual holdback to ensure satisfactory completion of work.
Wrong (C): Advance payments are prepayments or assets, not amounts withheld from billings for work already performed.
Wrong (D): A performance bond is a surety instrument, not an amount withheld from progress billings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12164;

-- Q12174: Payables and Accrued Liabilities [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recognized as miscellaneous income on the current-period income statement","Reversed against retained earnings as a prior-period equity adjustment","Carried as a liability indefinitely until claimed by the original payees","Remitted to the applicable state government under escheat law requirements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 405-20 and state escheat laws, entities holding dormant funds such as outstanding checks or customer credit balances must remit these amounts to the state after the statutory dormancy period expires. The obligation transfers to the state.
Wrong (A): Recognizing unclaimed property as income is generally improper; the obligation transfers from the payee to the state, not to earnings.
Wrong (B): Escheatment is not an equity adjustment; it is a legal remittance obligation governed by state statute.
Wrong (C): Entities cannot carry these amounts indefinitely; state law mandates remittance after the dormancy period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12174;

-- Q12181: Revenue Recognition [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As 365 separate performance obligations, each one satisfied at a specific point in time","As 12 separate performance obligations, with one obligation recognized each calendar month","As a single performance obligation under the series provision for substantially similar services","As two performance obligations split between weekday cleaning services and weekend cleaning services"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 606-10-25-14(b), a series of distinct goods or services that are substantially the same and have the same pattern of transfer is treated as a single performance obligation. Each day''s cleaning is distinct, and time elapsed measures progress identically for each service.
Wrong (A): The series guidance overrides individual treatment when both criteria (substantially the same, same transfer pattern) are met.
Wrong (B): Monthly grouping has no basis under the series provision; the standard evaluates the nature of the services, not calendar periods.
Wrong (D): Splitting by weekday versus weekend has no support under ASC 606 when all services share the same characteristics.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12181;

-- Q12183: Revenue Recognition [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$200,000, because the slotting fee is classified as a separate marketing expense","$215,000, because the slotting fee increases the total consideration exchanged between parties","$200,000, with the slotting fee disclosed separately in the financial statement notes","$185,000, because the slotting fee reduces the transaction price as a non-distinct payment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 606-10-32-25, consideration payable to a customer is accounted for as a reduction of the transaction price unless the payment is in exchange for a distinct good or service. Since the shelf space is not distinct, the $15,000 reduces the transaction price to $185,000.
Wrong (A): ASC 606 does not allow separate expense treatment when the consideration payable is not for a distinct good or service.
Wrong (B): The slotting fee is paid by the seller to the customer, so it reduces rather than increases the transaction price.
Wrong (C): Footnote-only disclosure does not satisfy the requirement to reduce the transaction price for non-distinct payments.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12183;

-- Q12184: Revenue Recognition [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A substantive reason exists, the product is identified as the customer''s, it is ready for transfer, and the entity cannot redirect it","The customer has made full payment and signed a nonrefundable and non-cancellable purchase agreement","The entity has obtained insurance on the product naming the customer as the designated beneficiary","The product has been segregated in a bonded warehouse with the customer holding the access key"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 606-10-25-30, bill-and-hold revenue recognition requires all four criteria: (1) substantive reason for the arrangement, (2) product separately identified as the customer''s, (3) product currently ready for physical transfer, and (4) entity cannot use or direct the product to another customer.
Wrong (B): Full payment alone does not satisfy the bill-and-hold criteria; all four conditions must be met regardless of payment status.
Wrong (C): Insurance naming the customer as beneficiary may support the arrangement but is not one of the four required criteria.
Wrong (D): Warehouse segregation may help demonstrate criterion (2), but alone it does not satisfy all four requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12184;

-- Q12185: Revenue Recognition [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Disclose the coupon as a contingent obligation and recognize the full $800 as revenue at sale","Allocate a portion of the $800 to the coupon as a separate performance obligation at sale","Recognize the coupon as a marketing expense in the period when it is actually redeemed","Defer the entire $800 of revenue until the coupon either expires or is redeemed by the customer"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 606-10-55-41 through 55-45, a customer option to acquire additional goods at a discount that provides a material right is treated as a separate performance obligation. The $800 transaction price must be allocated between the smartphone and the material right based on relative standalone selling prices.
Wrong (A): A material right is not merely a contingent disclosure item; it requires allocation of the transaction price.
Wrong (C): The coupon provides an incremental benefit not available to all customers, making it a performance obligation rather than a marketing cost.
Wrong (D): Deferring all revenue is excessive; only the portion allocated to the material right is deferred.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12185;

-- Q12188: Revenue Recognition [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As a lease, because the repurchase price is below the original selling price and the customer benefits from returning","As a sale with a separate put option valued at $20,000 recognized independently of the sale transaction","As a sale with full revenue of $400,000, because the call option is entirely at the entity''s discretion","As a financing arrangement with $400,000 recorded as a liability until the option period expires"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 606-10-55-70 through 55-72, when an entity has a call option to repurchase an asset at a price lower than the original selling price, the arrangement is accounted for as a lease if the customer has a significant economic incentive to return the asset. Since $380,000 is below $400,000 but exceeds the expected market value of $350,000, the customer benefits from returning it.
Wrong (B): The arrangement involves a call option held by the seller, not a put option; the repurchase analysis under ASC 606 applies.
Wrong (C): ASC 606 requires evaluating the economic substance of repurchase options regardless of which party holds the option.
Wrong (D): A financing arrangement applies when the repurchase price exceeds the original selling price, not when it is lower.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12188;

-- Q12195: Revenue Recognition [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Three separate performance obligations because each component has standalone value independently","Two performance obligations: the integration service bundled with one combined deliverable package","Three separate performance obligations with the integration service recognized ratably over time","One combined performance obligation because significant integration makes components inseparable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 606-10-25-21(b), even when goods or services are individually capable of providing benefit, they are not distinct if the entity provides a significant integration service that substantially modifies or customizes them. All items form a single combined performance obligation.
Wrong (A): The ''capable of being distinct'' criterion alone is insufficient; goods must also be ''separately identifiable'' within the contract context.
Wrong (B): There is no basis to separate the integration service from the deliverables when it is the integration that makes them inseparable.
Wrong (C): Recognizing integration ratably does not address the fundamental issue that the components are not distinct from each other.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12195;

-- Q12199: Revenue Recognition [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["When the end customer purchases a sofa, because control has not transferred under the consignment","When Fieldstone delivers the sofas to HomeStyle, because physical possession has been transferred","Ratably over the expected selling period of the consigned inventory at the showroom","When HomeStyle accepts the sofas and places them on the showroom floor for display"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 606-10-55-80, indicators of a consignment arrangement include the ability to return the product and lack of obligation to pay until goods are sold. Since HomeStyle can return unsold sofas and only pays upon sale, control remains with Fieldstone until end-customer purchase.
Wrong (B): Physical delivery alone does not transfer control when consignment indicators are present under ASC 606.
Wrong (C): Ratable recognition has no basis; revenue is recognized at the point in time when control transfers to the end customer.
Wrong (D): Acceptance and display do not constitute a transfer of control when the consignee has unlimited return rights.$EXPL$
